proofs.proof_tex:
The pointwise bounds established in \(\texttt{thm:causal-transfer}\), together with \(\texttt{ass:strict-slope}\) and \(\texttt{ass:variance}\), give, for every \(P\in\mathcal M\) and \(a\in J\),
\[
 \left(\frac{c_gc_\theta^2}{4V_\kappa}\right)^{1/3}
 \le
 \left\{\frac{f(a)\theta'(a)^2}{4\kappa(a)}\right\}^{1/3}
 \le
 \left(\frac{C_gC_\theta^2}{4v_\kappa}\right)^{1/3}. \tag{1}
\]
Integration over \(J\), followed by \(\texttt{def:spatial-clock}\), yields
\[
 |J|\left(\frac{c_gc_\theta^2}{4V_\kappa}\right)^{1/3}
 \le\mathcal V(P)\le
 |J|\left(\frac{C_gC_\theta^2}{4v_\kappa}\right)^{1/3}. \tag{2}
\]
Because \(J=[\delta,1-\delta]\) and \(0<\delta<1/2\), one has \(|J|=1-2\delta>0\).  Every remaining factor in the lower bound is positive.  Consequently,
\[
 \inf_{P\in\mathcal M}T_n(P)
 \ge n^{1/3}|J|\left(\frac{c_gc_\theta^2}{4V_\kappa}\right)^{1/3}
 \longrightarrow\infty. \tag{3}
\]

We now work under the two conditional premises explicitly stated in the theorem.  Let
\[
 b_n=b_{\mathcal E}(T_n(P)),
 \qquad
 \widehat b_n=b_{\mathcal E}(\widehat T_n).
\]
The clock definitions imply the exact ratio identity
\[
 \frac{\widehat T_n}{T_n(P)}
 =\frac{\widehat{\mathcal V}_n}{\mathcal V(P)}. \tag{4}
\]
The assumed uniform relative consistency, together with positivity and continuity of the logarithm at one, therefore gives
\[
 \log\frac{\widehat T_n}{T_n(P)}=o_P(1) \tag{5}
\]
uniformly over \(P\in\mathcal M\).  Equations (3)--(4) also imply that \(T_n(P)\) diverges uniformly and that \(\widehat T_n\) diverges uniformly in probability.

For \(v>0\),
\[
 \mathcal E'(v)=\beta+\frac{\gamma}{v}\longrightarrow\beta>0. \tag{6}
\]
Hence, with probability tending uniformly to one, both inverse centerings lie in the stipulated eventual interval on which \(\mathcal E\) is strictly increasing.  Since their images \(\log T_n(P)\) and \(\log\widehat T_n\) diverge, one also has \(b_n\to\infty\) uniformly and \(\widehat b_n\to\infty\) uniformly in probability.

On this event, the mean-value theorem supplies a point \(\widetilde b_n\) between \(b_n\) and \(\widehat b_n\) such that
\[
 \mathcal E'(\widetilde b_n)(\widehat b_n-b_n)
 =\mathcal E(\widehat b_n)-\mathcal E(b_n)
 =\log\frac{\widehat T_n}{T_n(P)}. \tag{7}
\]
Both endpoints diverge, so \(\widetilde b_n\to\infty\) uniformly in probability.  By the definition
\(
 a_{\mathcal E}(T)=1/\mathcal E'(b_{\mathcal E}(T))
\), equations (5)--(7) give
\[
 \frac{b_{\mathcal E}(\widehat T_n)-b_{\mathcal E}(T_n(P))}
 {a_{\mathcal E}(T_n(P))}
 =\frac{\mathcal E'(b_n)}{\mathcal E'(\widetilde b_n)}
 \log\frac{\widehat T_n}{T_n(P)}
 =o_P(1), \tag{8}
\]
because the derivative ratio converges uniformly in probability to \(\beta/\beta=1\).  Similarly,
\[
 \frac{a_{\mathcal E}(\widehat T_n)}{a_{\mathcal E}(T_n(P))}
 =\frac{\mathcal E'(b_n)}{\mathcal E'(\widehat b_n)}
 \longrightarrow1 \tag{9}
\]
uniformly.  The future-excursion premise provides only the contemplated interpretation of these expressions as a center and scale.  Equations (4)--(9) establish conditional plug-in equivalence, not clock consistency itself, an excursion law, a critical value, or coverage.
